## Important Design Principle

The interrupt controller should be independent from the actual peripherals.

It should not contain UART-specific or GPIO-specific logic.

Instead, it should receive generic interrupt signals:

    IRQ[0]
    IRQ[1]
    IRQ[2]
    IRQ[3]

The meaning of each interrupt source is defined by the SoC architecture.

For example:

    IRQ[0] → UART
    IRQ[1] → GPIO
    IRQ[2] → Timer
    IRQ[3] → Reserved

This makes the interrupt controller reusable.

---

## Why This Matters for the Final SoC

The final Linux computer will need interrupts.

Linux cannot efficiently operate a complex system by constantly polling every peripheral.

Instead, hardware events can generate interrupts.

For example:

    UART receives data
           ↓
    UART interrupt
           ↓
    Interrupt Controller
           ↓
    RISC-V CPU
           ↓
    Interrupt handler
           ↓
    Linux driver

This is a fundamental part of how operating systems communicate with hardware.

---

## Connection to RISC-V

The eventual CPU will be RISC-V.

A RISC-V system needs a mechanism for handling interrupts.

The exact final interrupt architecture may be different from this simplified controller.

A real RISC-V Linux system can use more advanced interrupt hardware such as a Platform-Level Interrupt Controller (PLIC) or other interrupt mechanisms depending on the system architecture.

This project is not intended to implement a complete PLIC.

Instead, it is a simplified model designed to understand the fundamental concepts:

    Peripheral
        ↓
    Interrupt Request
        ↓
    Interrupt Controller
        ↓
    CPU
        ↓
    Interrupt Handler

Understanding this simplified architecture will make the more advanced RISC-V interrupt architecture easier to understand later.

