## Reflection

This project introduced interrupts and showed how hardware can notify a CPU when an event requires attention.

The most important difference I learned was between polling and interrupts.

With polling, the CPU repeatedly checks hardware even when nothing has happened.

With interrupts, hardware can notify the CPU only when something requires attention.

I also learned why an interrupt controller is useful. Instead of connecting every peripheral directly to the CPU, the interrupt controller can collect requests from multiple peripherals and provide a structured interface to the CPU.

The pending and mask registers also demonstrate how software can control which hardware interrupts are enabled.

---

## Problems

One challenge was understanding the difference between an interrupt request and the final interrupt signal sent to the CPU.

A peripheral may generate an interrupt, but the interrupt controller can prevent it from reaching the CPU if that interrupt source is masked.

Another challenge was understanding how multiple interrupts can be active at the same time.

For example:

    IRQ = 0101

means that multiple peripherals are requesting attention simultaneously.

The controller therefore needs to keep track of the individual interrupt sources rather than only storing a single interrupt bit.

Another important challenge was understanding how interrupt clearing works.

An interrupt should not simply disappear because the CPU noticed it. The underlying event must be handled and the interrupt state must be cleared appropriately.

---

## What I Would Improve

The current interrupt controller is intentionally simple.

Possible future improvements include:

- Interrupt priorities
- Edge-triggered interrupts
- More interrupt sources
- Separate enable and pending registers
- Interrupt acknowledgement
- Nested interrupts
- Interrupt vector support
- Per-interrupt configuration
- More advanced RISC-V interrupt support
- PLIC-like functionality

The final SoC will require a more realistic interrupt architecture than this simple educational controller.

